592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122889 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a8388c8-14c8-526e-897e-3e8763825c6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122890 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122890 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122890 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "227d9063-f6e1-5d7c-9d60-6e1248921fca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122891 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122891 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122891 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "28cf6df6-bbd0-54e6-8db1-cc6003cdc8fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122892 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122892 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122892 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b3f4c88-03dd-5ffd-9ed0-a52e342ffc9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122893 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122893 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186122893 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "566ffd59-7879-578b-801c-c0308913ddcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124339 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5502" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124339 #edb/ref 1071 "Find $A^{100},$ where \n![](images/81/81d78f5e129614d85d3719539ab9afc1a04307afb3b03eb9d7a55829ee6354c9.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124339 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfb93cc8-6bf0-59d4-8fbc-c94f5c0c1a69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124339 #edb/ref 1074 "First,  notice that\n\n![](images/43/43438bad181467e28a2e0758f7b7aa070fd5f5de37e1a6e19d4cabb591dc4f7d.svg)\n\nSo, we have been given the following diagonalization of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/93/93e5e9f9947fb4299da165192f103d7be7494ecc752edacf1a4931d4766337ef.svg)\n\nSince $A=PDP^{-1},$ we can compute $A^k$ by using the formula \n\n$$\nA^{k} = PD^{k}P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{100} & =P{D}^{100}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{100}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{1}^{100} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {0}^{100}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124340 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124340 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the eigenvectors of the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $E,$ given that \n![](images/06/063c1f52097f096bc39a2f8ba29f251c655c926a542828dfba50bcd3bb29dcea.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124340 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "737021ad-5198-564f-bbe2-cc5a06ee5a82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124340 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E\\mathbin{:}$ \n![](images/84/84fd9d7243958aa329a23c3c9f19e7c35b8969699511b72bdfc4546b4577fc4a.svg)\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are $\\lambda_1=3$ and $\\lambda_2=6$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are\n\n![](images/fd/fd405e80c68f63df281c9f3e2fe4dd8ab25e32a0214619a7e5220c7e01944ab8.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124381 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124381 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $C$ that has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-4,$ and corresponding eigenvectors\n\n![](images/e3/e38c742a22bea651a3c410744f798297306661b6eee2929d7c5efff3bdb78844.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124381 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c1ebc3ac-7a26-565f-9798-9de531dc6e47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124381 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $\\mathcal{B}= \\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of the eigenvectors of $C,$ then $C$ must be diagonalizable.\n\nSo, we can write $C=PDP^{-1},$ where $P$ is a matrix whose columns are the eigenvectors of $C,$ and $D$ is a diagonal matrix whose entries are the eigenvalues in the corresponding order: \n\n![](images/eb/ebedba78b53f92ed5e4d69322a5e682675bdfc13b2687575fdf94f9c78a1cbe5.svg)\n\nLet ![](images/16/16933b8fd024b62ad8fafa36b382aefce74383a9bf47b952bf7e0e12fe26cae6.svg) Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, carrying out the necessary matrix multiplication, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272030 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186379803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186388653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186394724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1039 "Raising a Matrix to a Large Power Using the Properties of Diagonalization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124530 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "634729e9-7633-52d8-99fa-b00fc9cbe96a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125048 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272031 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272032 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323905 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186402702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186404149 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427864 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a Matrix Using the Properties of Diagonalization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124531 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125058 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186351264 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186392121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186427863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1039 "Restoring a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124572 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "50afb9e4-b28b-5394-bc2b-eff9549e4a9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1045 "Properties of Diagonalization" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1050 840.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124754 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "95082e0a-322a-5587-9f1a-1280f50a5124" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125048 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186122888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125048 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186402530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125048 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125048 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125048 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $E$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]E=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125048 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "18390ea4-94e2-564d-880f-13ae6fda894d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125048 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), left-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $E:$\n\n$$\nE={{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $E$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $E$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P.$ Computing $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the corresponding eigenvectors are: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125085 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186392115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125085 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125085 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125085 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${A}^{97},$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125085 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7259ad7-f6a1-5f38-87c9-86ba246db7b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125085 #edb/ref 1074 "We have been given the following diagonalization of $A.$\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $A=PD{P}^{-1}$, we can compute ${A}^{k}$ by using the formula \n\n$$\n{A}^{k}=P{D}^{k}{P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the inverse of the matrix $P.$ We get\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{-2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCarrying out the necessary multiplication, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{97} & =P{D}^{97}{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{97}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{97} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {1}^{97}\\end{aligned}](-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125419 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125419 #edb/ref 1061 20121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125419 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "934d1edd-bf1c-5c1f-8924-ede352d7d23b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125419 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125420 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125420 #edb/ref 1061 20122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125420 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d76255af-54d9-5dcb-9a9c-7b30b6c4a98a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125420 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125421 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125421 #edb/ref 1061 20123 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125421 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ed440e4-0b21-56bd-92c6-0ef75f28a68c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125421 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125422 #edb/ref 1061 20124 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125422 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "476486fe-26df-57a3-89ab-80eeb17483b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125422 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125492 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125492 #edb/ref 1061 26234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125492 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1265d446-04a7-5d76-a5b8-e7c56bb1b4c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125492 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125790 #edb/ref 1064 "One useful property of diagonalization is that it allows us to easily determine the power of a matrix.\n\nAs an example, we consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix ![](images/53/53b7a9efd9f2ae1ef4adc7cee5f86f784722777b8c29f268dbd0e822ff1354f9.svg) with the diagonalization\n![](images/e3/e3111a91b80d238cf5255eb9a9884dcd3f80e914039c5e789e32c07b55cf5b06.svg)\n\nWe will use the diagonalization of $A$ to compute $A^{55}.$\n\nLet's start by computing $A^2.$ Using the properties of matrix multiplication, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =PD{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =PD\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P(DD){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $A^3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot A \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P({D}^{2}D){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{3}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix $P$ and its inverse $P^{-1}$ will always cancel each other out in the middle of the multiplication! So by induction, we have that\n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}55}=P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}55} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we only need to find $D^{\\color{blue}55}.$\n\nSince $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {D}^{3} & ={D}^{2}D=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing this pattern, we get\n\n![](images/05/05e10fdc6944077a48b0214a41fae8d418a1b66c8d87a47d652158c84285c246.svg)\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{55} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{55}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given that $A = PDP^{-1},$ for any non-negative integer ${\\color{blue}k},$ we obtain \n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}k} = P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}k} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125790 #edb/ref 1065 3576 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125790 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b7628bf0-02d4-514e-a564-669610253cd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125790 #edb/ref 1067 "Raising a Diagonalizable Matrix to a Power" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125791 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that the diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ and corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ is\n![](images/cd/cde63e04857414720bed0cb247e1e0c4bae76b69823f4aeb494cda13c139ac13.svg)\n\nThis idea also works in reverse. Given a diagonalization $A$ in the form \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe can immediately deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are the diagonal entries of $D,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of $P.$ This is because the diagonalization of a matrix is (almost) unique.\n\n**Note:** The diagonalization is \"almost\" unique because the order of the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs may vary, and the eigenvectors may be replaced with scalar multiples of themselves. However, none of these variations change the actual outcome regarding the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs that we deduce." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125791 #edb/ref 1065 3